- **The inner layers do no I/O.** The code of `domain`, `protocol`, `application` and `presentation`, and of their
  levels, imports nothing that reads or writes outside the program (files, the network, processes, the terminal, the
  system around it), and `rotproof check` fails on an import of one of these, through every spelling the file binds it
  by (an alias, a name taken from it, a rename). The table is Rotproof's, and a project cannot change it. `infrastructure`,
  `composition`, `ui`, the entry file and {tests} are not judged.
{table}  What fails is the entry of the table, whatever name is used under it, and the finding says each name it found.
{names}  **The I/O goes in `infrastructure.outbound`, behind a port in `protocol.outbound` that the use case is given:** the
  use case passes the values on to `domain`, and a screen's model (`presentation.models`, which imports only
  `protocol.inbound`) gets what it needs from its page{hook}, or through a use case it is given.{passed} Approving the import,
  renaming or respelling it (an alias, a string, a module held in a variable), listing it under `allowed` in
  the declaration (which is for packages, below), moving the code to a layer that is not judged (`infrastructure`, `composition`, `ui`), or
  moving the code to a path in `unchecked` or `moving` is not a fix. A finding that cannot be fixed now is approved as any
  forbidden import is, with `rotproof approve <file> <entry> --reason "<why>"` (the user asks for it): the entry is the
  one the finding names (`shutil`, `urllib.request`, `std::fs`, `std::os::fs`, `std::io`, `fs`, `bun:`{keys}), and one
  approval covers every use of it in that file; `rotproof check` prints each approval with the names the file now uses
  under the entry.
{allowed}